    <node id="paper.bireal.eccv2018">
      <data key="label">Bi-Real Net (ECCV 2018 / IJCV)</data>
      <data key="type">Paper</data>
      <data key="summary">1-bit CNN with full-precision identity shortcuts around every binary conv (≈2× real-valued paths vs BinaryNet). ApproxSign piecewise-polynomial STE. ImageNet ResNet-18: 56.4% top-1 / 79.5% top-5 (vs XNOR-Net 51.2% / BinaryNet 42.2% / FP ResNet-18 ~69.3%). ResNet-34: 62.2% top-1 / 83.9% top-5. Codes: github.com/liuzechun/Bi-Real-net. Lab uses Bi-Real residuals on MNIST/CIFAR proxies; full ImageNet train is ACCEPTED-NON-GOAL.</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.95</data>
      <data key="status">merged</data>
      <data key="sources">https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.00278 | https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.01335 | docs/02_SOTA_SURVEY.md | docs/03_FAILURE_ANALYSIS.md</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paper.real2bin.iclr2020">
      <data key="label">Real-to-Binary Network (ICLR 2020)</data>
      <data key="type">Paper</data>
      <data key="summary">Martinez et al. train binary ResNet-18 via progressive attention matching to a real teacher + data-driven gating/re-scaling of binary-conv outputs using pre-binarization real activations. ImageNet top-1 **65.4%** / top-5 **86.2%** (strong baseline alone 60.9%); FP ResNet-18 69.3% → ~3.9 pp gap. CIFAR-100 Real-to-Bin** 76.2%. arXiv:2003.11535; OpenReview BJg4NgBKvH; code github.com/brais-martinez/real2binary. Often cited loosely as 'Real-to-Bin' without ID — strengthen all lab refs to this paper.</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.95</data>
      <data key="status">verified</data>
      <data key="sources">https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.11535 | https://openreview.net/forum?id=BJg4NgBKvH | https://github.com/brais-martinez/real2binary</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paper.reactnet.eccv2020">
      <data key="label">ReActNet (ECCV 2020)</data>
      <data key="type">Paper</data>
      <data key="summary">Generalizes Sign→RSign and PReLU→RPReLU (learnable channel-wise thresholds/slopes) + distributional KL loss vs real teacher. MobileNetV1-based ReActNet-A: **69.4%** ImageNet top-1 at **0.87×10^8 OPs** (~3.0 pp below real MobileNet twin 72.4%). ReActNet-B 70.1% @ 1.63e8 OPs; ReActNet-C 71.4% @ 2.14e8 OPs. ReAct on Bi-Real-18 → 65.5% (+9.1 pp). Comparison anchors: XNOR 51.2%, Bi-Real-18 56.4%, Real-to-Binary 65.4%, MeliusNet29 65.8%. Two-step training (acts then W+A). Code: github.com/liuzechun/ReActNet. Lab docs say '~71% claimed' — precise figures: A=69.4, C=71.4.</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.96</data>
      <data key="status">merged</data>
      <data key="sources">https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.03488 | https://github.com/liuzechun/ReActNet | docs/02_SOTA_SURVEY.md</data>
    </node>
    <node id="metric.imagenet.bnn_ladder">
      <data key="label">ImageNet 1-bit CNN accuracy ladder</data>
      <data key="type">Metric</data>
      <data key="summary">Canonical ImageNet top-1 ladder for W1A1 (unless noted): BinaryNet 42.2% → XNOR-Net 51.2% → Bi-Real-18 56.4% → Real-to-Binary 65.4% → ReActNet-A 69.4% / ReActNet-C 71.4%. OPs metric = BOPs/64 + FLOPs. Lab should cite this ladder instead of vague 'within 3% of FP MobileNet' alone.</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.94</data>
      <data key="status">verified</data>
      <data key="sources">https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.03488 | https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.11535 | https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.00278</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paper.bitnet.2023">
      <data key="label">BitNet (1-bit Transformer, 2023)</data>
      <data key="type">Paper</data>
      <data key="summary">Wang et al. replace nn.Linear with BitLinear (1-bit weights, higher-bit acts). Foundational proof that Transformers can train with extreme weight quantization. arXiv:2310.11453. Superseded for quality by ternary BitNet b1.58; keep distinction: original BitNet ≈ ±1, b1.58 adds 0.</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.9</data>
      <data key="status">merged</data>
      <data key="sources">https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.11453 | docs/02_SOTA_SURVEY.md</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paper.bitnet_b158.2024">
      <data key="label">BitNet b1.58 (Ma et al., 2024)</data>
      <data key="type">Paper</data>
      <data key="summary">Ternary weights {-1,0,1} via absmean RoundClip; 8-bit activations; LLaMA-alike (RMSNorm, SwiGLU, RoPE, no bias). At equal size+100B RedPajama tokens: 3B BitNet matches FP16 LLaMA twin (PPL 9.91 vs 10.04; avg zero-shot 50.2 vs 49.7) with **3.55×** less memory and **2.71×** lower latency (FasterTransformer+2-bit Ladder kernel on GPU). 70B: **11×** max batch → **8.9×** throughput vs FP16 on 2×A100. Arithmetic energy model: ~**71.4×** lower matmul energy on 7nm (INT8 add vs FP16 mul/add) — chip model, not SoC. Equivalence heuristic: 13B b1.58 cheaper than 3B FP16; 30B vs 7B; 70B vs 13B. Caveat: headline GPU latency/memory from research kernels; production CPU path is bitnet.cpp.</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.95</data>
      <data key="status">merged</data>
      <data key="sources">https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.17764 | docs/02_SOTA_SURVEY.md | docs/14_HARDWARE_AND_ENERGY.md</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paper.bitnet_a48.2024">
      <data key="label">BitNet a4.8 (4-bit activations, 2024)</data>
      <data key="type">Paper</data>
      <data key="summary">Wang/Ma/Wei hybrid: INT4 (or FP4) activations into attention/FFN inputs; TopK sparsify + INT8 on intermediate states (attn out-proj, FFN down). Continue-train from W1.58A8 → W1.58A4 (e.g. 95B+5B tokens). Matches b1.58 quality at same token budget; ~**55%** activated params (7B: 3.4B active); supports **3-bit KV** with negligible avg loss. Enables INT4/FP4 kernels. arXiv:2411.04965. Follow-on: BitNet v2 (arXiv:2504.18415) native 4-bit acts + Hadamard.</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.92</data>
      <data key="status">merged</data>
      <data key="sources">https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.04965 | https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.18415</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paper.bitnet_cpp.2024">
      <data key="label">bitnet.cpp / 1-bit AI Infra (2024–2025)</data>
      <data key="type">Paper</data>
      <data key="summary">Wang et al. arXiv:2410.16144 — ternary CPU kernels (TL1/TL2) vs llama.cpp FP16. **Cite carefully with device+thread context:** Apple M2 Ultra unlimited threads: speedup **1.37×–5.07×** (125M→70B); Intel i7-13700H: **2.37×–6.17×**. Energy J/token savings: M2 **55.4%/64.6%/70.0%** (700M/7B/70B); i7 **71.9%/82.2%** (700M/7B). 100B ternary on M2 ~6.58 tok/s unlimited (human-reading class). Lossless vs FP32 kernel on WildChat token match (TL1/TL2 100% vs TQ1_0 1.4%). ACL follow-up arXiv:2502.11880. Official HF: microsoft/bitnet-b1.58-2B-4T (+gguf). Lab docs cite ranges correctly; add hardware footnotes + J/token table.</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.96</data>
      <data key="status">merged</data>
      <data key="sources">https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.16144 | https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.11880 | https://github.com/microsoft/BitNet | https://hf.co/microsoft/bitnet-b1.58-2B-4T | docs/23_BITNET_CPP_BRIDGE.md</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paper.bitdistill.2025">
      <data key="label">BitNet Distillation / BitDistill (2025)</data>
      <data key="type">Paper</data>
      <data key="summary">Wu et al. arXiv:2510.13998 — convert off-the-shelf FP LLMs (Qwen3 0.6–4B) to 1.58-bit for **task-specific** finetune without full BitNet pretrain. Three stages: (1) SubLN before MHSA/FFN out-projections; (2) continue-pretrain ~10B Falcon tokens; (3) CE + KL logits distill (τ=5) + MiniLM-style multi-head attention-relation distill (prefer **single late layer**). Naive BitNet-SFT gaps ~13–15 pp and worsen with scale; BitDistill recovers FP16-SFT parity on MNLI/QNLI/SST-2 and CNNDM ROUGE while claiming ~**10×** memory and **~2.65×** CPU tok/s (paper table: 427→1135 tok/s, 1.20→0.11 G). Complementary to GPTQ/AWQ init. Distinct from BitDistiller (arXiv:2402.10631, sub-4-bit self-distill).</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.93</data>
      <data key="status">merged</data>
      <data key="sources">https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.13998 | https://hf.co/papers/2510.13998 | docs/13_TRAINING_QAT_DISTILL.md</data>
    </node>
    <node id="recipe.qat_158bit">
      <data key="label">1.58-bit QAT / distill recipe checklist</data>
      <data key="type">Method</data>
      <data key="summary">Production-leaning path: (A) From-scratch BitNet b1.58 if you control pretrain tokens (≥100B–2T) and need general LLM. (B) BitDistill for downstream: SubLN → short CPT → logits+attention distill; do **not** absmean-PTQ a chat Llama and ship. (C) Gradual λ / STE with absmean ternary + INT8 acts. Keep stem/embed/LM-head higher precision unless proven otherwise. Prefer bitnet.cpp TL kernels for CPU serve. Lab TernaryLinear is pedagogy/size only.</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.9</data>
      <data key="status">verified</data>
      <data key="sources">https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.13998 | https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.17764 | docs/23_BITNET_CPP_BRIDGE.md | docs/13_TRAINING_QAT_DISTILL.md</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paper.bibert.2022">
      <data key="label">BiBERT (fully binarized BERT, 2022)</data>
      <data key="type">Paper</data>
      <data key="summary">Qin et al. arXiv:2203.06390 — first accurate **W1 E1 A1** BERT. Failure diagnosis: (1) attention-weight information collapse under direct Sign; (2) distillation direction mismatch when using products of two binary activations. Fixes: **Bi-Attention** (maximize entropy of binary attention reps) + **Direction-Matching Distillation (DMD)**. Claims **56.3×** FLOPs and **31.2×** size savings; beats BinaryBERT W1A1 by ~20.4 pp avg GLUE and Q2BERT W2A8 by ~13.3 pp. Lab docs mention BiBERT briefly — strengthen with these failure modes and numbers.</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.92</data>
      <data key="status">merged</data>
      <data key="sources">https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.06390 | https://github.com/htqin/BiBERT | docs/13_TRAINING_QAT_DISTILL.md</data>
    </node>
    <node id="concept.binary_transformers">
      <data key="label">Binary / ternary transformers landscape</data>
      <data key="type">Concept</data>
      <data key="summary">Three distinct lines: (1) BiBERT-style full W/A/E binarization of encoder BERT (hardest — attention). (2) BitNet Transformer: BitLinear replaces Linear; attention softmax/KV usually higher bit. (3) BitNet b1.58 / a4.8 / BitDistill for generative LLMs. Do not conflate GLUE BiBERT wins with LLM chat quality. Lab tiny binary-ViT sketch is pedagogical only.</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.9</data>
      <data key="status">verified</data>
      <data key="sources">https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.06390 | https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.11453 | https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.17764 | docs/13_TRAINING_QAT_DISTILL.md</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paper.finn.fpga2017">
      <data key="label">FINN FPGA BNN framework (FPGA 2017+)</data>
      <data key="type">Paper</data>
      <data key="summary">Umuroglu et al. streaming heterogeneous dataflow for BNNs on Zynq. Optimizations: XNOR+popcount MVTU; BN+sign→thresholds; maxpool→OR. ZC706 @~200 MHz wall power: **SFC-max MNIST 12.3M FPS**, 0.31 µs latency, Pchip 7.3 W / Pwall 21.2 W → **~583k FPS/W wall** / **~1.69M FPS/W chip**; LFC-max 1.56M FPS; **CNV-max CIFAR-10/SVHN 21.9k FPS**, 283 µs, Pchip 3.6 W / Pwall 11.7 W → **~1.87k FPS/W wall**. Modern FINN: Brevitas→ONNX→FINN IR→HLS/RTL; github.com/Xilinx/finn. Lab docs cite FPS class correctly; add FPS/W and power rails.</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.93</data>
      <data key="status">merged</data>
      <data key="sources">https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.07119 | https://xilinx.github.io/finn/ | https://github.com/Xilinx/finn | docs/14_HARDWARE_AND_ENERGY.md</data>
    </node>
    <node id="tool.larq_lce">
      <data key="label">Larq + Larq Compute Engine</data>
      <data key="type">Tool</data>
      <data key="summary">TF/Keras BNN training (Larq) + TFLite custom ops for ARM BGEMM (LCE). Hand-tuned tiling/vectorization/multithreading; documented 8.5–18.5× class wins on Pixel/RPi vs FP baselines in ecosystem surveys. Upstream Larq archived ~2026 — pin commit / vendor fork. Best stock path for mobile **CPU** W+A binary CNNs. Not a BitNet LLM stack.</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.88</data>
      <data key="status">merged</data>
      <data key="sources">https://github.com/larq/compute-engine | https://docs.larq.dev/ | docs/16_ECOSYSTEM_AND_TOOLING.md</data>
    </node>
    <node id="tool.brevitas_finn">
      <data key="label">Brevitas → FINN (QAT→FPGA)</data>
      <data key="type">Tool</data>
      <data key="summary">Brevitas (Xilinx/AMD): PyTorch QAT/PTQ with per-tensor bitwidth control including 1-bit; QuantConv/Linear/MHA/RNN. Export ONNX into FINN for FPGA dataflow. End-to-end: train in Brevitas → tidy/streamline → HW layers → fold for FPS target → Vivado/PYNQ deploy. Preferred industrial vision edge path when FPGA available. Heavy vs this repo's teaching kernels.</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.9</data>
      <data key="status">merged</data>
      <data key="sources">https://github.com/Xilinx/brevitas | https://finn-dev.readthedocs.io/en/latest/end_to_end_flow.html | docs/16_ECOSYSTEM_AND_TOOLING.md</data>
    </node>
    <node id="decision.awq_gptq_vs_binary">
      <data key="label">When AWQ/GPTQ/torchao win vs binary/ternary</data>
      <data key="type">Decision</data>
      <data key="summary">GPU datacenter LLM serve (2024–2026): prefer FP8/INT4 via torchao, AWQ, GPTQ, vLLM/TensorRT — Tensor Cores beat research XNOR. AWQ protects salient weights via activation magnitude (~1% channels); GPTQ second-order weight fit — GPTQ can overfit calib domain more than AWQ. CPU/edge LLM: BitNet b1.58 + bitnet.cpp. Mobile NPU: stock INT8/INT4, not XNOR. Vision MCU/FPGA fixed topology: FINN/Larq binary. Hybrid: PTQ INT4 for GPU API + ternary FFN on CPU rare; usually pick one stack. Binary rarely wins end-to-end on NVIDIA TC GPUs (arXiv:1911.04477).</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.91</data>
      <data key="status">merged</data>
      <data key="sources">https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.00978 | https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.17323 | docs/16_ECOSYSTEM_AND_TOOLING.md | docs/02_SOTA_SURVEY.md | docs/18_DECISION_TREE_AND_COMPLETE_ROADMAP.md</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paper.sparse_bitnet.2026">
      <data key="label">Sparse-BitNet (N:M + 1.58-bit, 2026)</data>
      <data key="type">Paper</data>
      <data key="summary">Zhang et al. arXiv:2603.05168 — ternary BitNet naturally ~42% zeros; more N:M-friendly than BF16. Sparse-BitLinear: magnitude mask from master weights each step, quant-then-mask, **dual STE** (gradients through masked weights). At 2:4, BF16 +18.8% norm PPL vs BitNet +5.7%; BitNet collapses later under N:8 sweeps. Custom 6:8 sparse TC kernels: up to **~1.30×** e2e on Qwen2.5-3B. Related: Q-Sparse (arXiv:2407.10969). Lab mentions Sparse-BitNet once — add these deltas.</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.9</data>
      <data key="status">merged</data>
      <data key="sources">https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.05168 | https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.10969 | docs/02_SOTA_SURVEY.md</data>
    </node>
    <node id="concept.mobile_npu_quant_reality">
      <data key="label">Mobile NPU INT8/INT4 reality (not 1-bit)</data>
      <data key="type">Concept</data>
      <data key="summary">Qualcomm HTP/QNN, Apple ANE/CoreML, Arm Ethos-U: document INT8/INT4/FP16 paths — **no stock 1-bit XNOR**. Ternary BitNet on Snapdragon needs custom Hexagon kernels (ENERZAi/QCS6490 class reports). Product tree: phone NPU → INT8; classic BNN CNN → CPU LCE / FPGA FINN; ternary LLM → bitnet.cpp or custom DSP. Aligns with lab docs/20 CLOSED-BY-PROXY.</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.9</data>
      <data key="status">merged</data>
      <data key="sources">docs/20_NPU_VENDOR_CLOSURE.md | docs/14_HARDWARE_AND_ENERGY.md</data>
    </node>
    <node id="failure.attention_binarization">
      <data key="label">Failure: attention binarization</data>
      <data key="type">FailureMode</data>
      <data key="summary">Sign on Q/K/attention maps destroys selective focus (BiBERT analysis: attention-weight information nearly collapses). Distilling using products of two binary activations causes STE direction mismatch (DMD needed). Practical mitigations: keep attention scores / softmax / KV higher bit (BitNet default); Bi-Attention+DMD for full binary BERT; a4.8 uses sparse INT8 intermediates on attn out-proj. Lab F8 Amdahl already notes softmax — elevate attention-specific citations.</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.92</data>
      <data key="status">verified</data>
      <data key="sources">https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.06390 | https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.04965 | docs/03_FAILURE_ANALYSIS.md</data>
    </node>
    <node id="failure.small_k_layers">
      <data key="label">Failure: small-K / thin layers</data>
      <data key="type">FailureMode</data>
      <data key="summary">Packed XNOR–popcount amortizes poorly when inner dim K ≪ 64/128 or layers are stem/head-sized: packing overhead + FP residual ops dominate; measured wall-clock can be &lt;1× vs FP BLAS. Standard recipe: leave first/last layers FP (≥8-bit); only binarize wide matmuls. Lab G15/G18 document re-pack and Python overhead — connect to K-threshold guidance in kernels docs.</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.88</data>
      <data key="status">verified</data>
      <data key="sources">docs/03_FAILURE_ANALYSIS.md | docs/34_COMPUTE_SPEEDUP.md | docs/06_CALCULATED_SPEEDUP_MODEL.md</data>
    </node>
    <node id="failure.bn_sensitivity">
      <data key="label">Failure: BatchNorm sensitivity in BNNs</data>
      <data key="type">FailureMode</data>
      <data key="summary">Without BN after binary layers, training almost always fails (discrete noisy acts). Running stats/momentum matter: Larq guides often use momentum ≈0.9. Prefer Adam on latent weights. On FPGA FINN, BN+sign folds to thresholds — training-time BN still required. Lab G7 CLOSED; keep as KG failure node with Larq/FINN citations.</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.9</data>
      <data key="status">verified</data>
      <data key="sources">docs/03_FAILURE_ANALYSIS.md | docs/09_GAP_REGISTER.md | https://docs.larq.dev/ | https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.07119</data>
    </node>
    <node id="failure.ptq_to_ternary">
      <data key="label">Failure: naive PTQ into ternary BitNet</data>
      <data key="type">FailureMode</data>
      <data key="summary">Absmean-PTQ or direct QAT of chat Llama into {-1,0,1} without SubLN/CPT/distill is unstable; BitDistill shows gaps ~14 pp that **widen** with scale (0.6B→4B). Sub-4-bit PTQ (GPTQ/AWQ alone) also collapses quality. Use BitDistill recipe or train BitNet from scratch. Lab F13 / docs/23 already warn — cite 2510.13998 numbers.</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.93</data>
      <data key="status">merged</data>
      <data key="sources">https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.13998 | docs/23_BITNET_CPP_BRIDGE.md | docs/17_EVALUATION_ROBUSTNESS_ECONOMICS.md</data>
    </node>
    <node id="artifact.hf_bitnet_2b">
      <data key="label">HF microsoft/bitnet-b1.58-2B-4T</data>
      <data key="type">System</data>
      <data key="summary">Official 2.4B BitNet b1.58 trained 4T tokens (arXiv:2504.12285). Variants: safetensors, bf16, gguf. Primary public checkpoint for bitnet.cpp demos. Prefer this over unofficial PTQ '1-bit' conversions.</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.94</data>
      <data key="status">merged</data>
      <data key="sources">https://hf.co/microsoft/bitnet-b1.58-2B-4T | https://hf.co/microsoft/bitnet-b1.58-2B-4T-gguf | https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.12285</data>
    </node>
    <node id="citation.strengthen.lab_weak">
      <data key="label">Lab citation strengthen list</data>
      <data key="type">Decision</data>
      <data key="summary">Papers named in docs without stable IDs or precise numbers: Real-to-Binary → arXiv:2003.11535 + 65.4%; ReActNet → 2003.03488 + 69.4/71.4; Bi-Real → 1808.00278 + 56.4/62.2; BitDistill → 2510.13998 (not BitDistiller 2402.10631); bitnet.cpp energy → 2410.16144 tables with CPU model; FINN → 1612.07119 + FPS/W; BiBERT → 2203.06390; Sparse-BitNet → 2603.05168; BitNet a4.8 → 2411.04965. Prefer these IDs in KG edges over 'Microsoft reports'.</data>
      <data key="confidence">0.95</data>
      <data key="status">verified</data>
      <data key="sources">docs/02_SOTA_SURVEY.md | docs/13_TRAINING_QAT_DISTILL.md | docs/14_HARDWARE_AND_ENERGY.md</data>
    </node>
